# Line endings.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) and runs on Linux runners. Without
# these rules, git checks shell scripts out with CRLF locally, and a script whose shebang ends
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails with an unreadable "bad interpreter" error -- or, worse, runs
# far enough to be confusing. Storage was always LF; this pins the WORKING COPY too, so the file
# a developer executes is byte-identical to the one CI executes.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Maestro flows and the PowerSync config are read by Linux containers and by the Maestro CLI's
# YAML parser. YAML tolerates CRLF, but the flows are also grepped and diffed in CI logs, and a
# mixed-ending tree makes those diffs unreadable.
.maestro/**     text eol=lf
e2e/**          text eol=lf

# Gradle wrappers are executed directly on the runner.
gradlew         text eol=lf

# Workflow YAML isn't read by anything CRLF-sensitive, but two branches adding the same file
# with different line endings is an add/add merge conflict over bytes that are otherwise
# identical -- exactly what happened to android-apk.yml between main and this branch.
.github/**      text eol=lf

# Binary, so git must never try to translate anything.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.apk           binary
*.keystore      binary
